drm_print: make drm_err()/drm_warn() messages visible on FreeBSD

__drm_dev_vprintk() formats every message through %pV, which FreeBSD's kernel
printf does not implement -- sys/kern/subr_prf.c has no 'V' case at all, and
linuxkpi maps printk() straight to printf(). So the "[drm]" prefix prints and
the entire message body is silently dropped.

Measured on an arm64 guest: 16 consecutive errors during X cursor activity
produced exactly this in dmesg, with nothing between them --

    [drm] *ERROR* [drm] *ERROR* [drm] *ERROR* ...

od(1) confirmed the buffer holds only the repeated prefix: no message, and not
even the stray hex pointer + literal 'V' that a bare %p would have left. Every
drm_err()/drm_warn()/drm_info() call in the tree is affected, so no DRM
diagnostic has ever been readable here.

DRM_ERROR() is unaffected: this same file's __drm_err() already carries a
__FreeBSD__ branch doing printf() + vprintf() instead of %pV. Do the same in
__drm_dev_vprintk().

--- a/drivers/gpu/drm/drm_print.c
+++ b/drivers/gpu/drm/drm_print.c
@@ -204,6 +204,23 @@
 	if (!prefix)
 		prefix = "";
 
+#ifdef __FreeBSD__
+	/*
+	 * FreeBSD's printf(9) implements no %pV -- sys/kern/subr_prf.c has no
+	 * 'V' case at all -- so passing vaf through it drops the entire message
+	 * body and leaves a bare "[drm]" prefix behind.  Format it the way
+	 * __drm_err() in this file already does for FreeBSD: emit the prefix,
+	 * then let vprintf() expand the caller's format with its own args.  The
+	 * caller's fmt carries its own trailing newline, exactly as on Linux.
+	 */
+	if (origin != NULL)
+		printf("%s[" DRM_NAME ":%p]%s%s ", level, origin, prefix_pad,
+		       prefix);
+	else
+		printf("%s[" DRM_NAME "]%s%s ", level, prefix_pad, prefix);
+	vprintf(vaf->fmt, *vaf->va);
+	return;
+#else
 	if (dev) {
 		if (origin)
 			dev_printk(level, dev, "[" DRM_NAME ":%ps]%s%s %pV",
@@ -219,6 +236,7 @@
 			printk("%s" "[" DRM_NAME "]%s%s %pV",
 			       level, prefix_pad, prefix, vaf);
 	}
+#endif
 }
 
 void __drm_printfn_info(struct drm_printer *p, struct va_format *vaf)
